  "directions": "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n  <meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n  <title>Arduino Lesson</title>\n  <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"../../css/exercise-directions.css\">\n</head>\n<body>\n  <h1>Exercise 1337: The Signal</h1>\n  <p><em>Something is listening on pin 13. It only answers to one number.</em></p>\n\n  <h2>Your Mission</h2>\n  <p>Transmit the digits <b>1</b>, <b>3</b>, <b>3</b>, <b>7</b> by blinking the LED on pin 13.</p>\n  <ol>\n    <li>In <span class=\"code\">setup()</span>: start Serial at 9600 and make pin 13 an <span class=\"code\">OUTPUT</span>.</li>\n    <li>For <b>each digit</b>, in order:\n      <ol>\n        <li><span class=\"code\">Serial.println</span> the digit.</li>\n        <li>Blink pin 13 that many times. One blink = <b>HIGH</b>, wait <b>200 ms</b>, <b>LOW</b>, wait <b>200 ms</b>.</li>\n        <li>After the last blink, wait an extra <b>1000 ms</b> before the next digit (a separate <span class=\"code\">delay(1000)</span>).</li>\n      </ol>\n    </li>\n  </ol>\n  <p>That's it. Run and Grade. If the signal is correct... you'll know.</p>\n\n  <h2>Hints</h2>\n  <ol class=\"hints\">\n    <li>Writing the blink code 14 times works, but a function like <span class=\"code\">void blinkDigit(int count)</span> with a <span class=\"code\">for</span> loop inside is much shorter.</li>\n    <li>Then <span class=\"code\">loop()</span> is only four lines.</li>\n    <li>When it's correct, keep an eye on the <b>Output</b> tab.</li>\n  </ol>\n</body>\n</html>\n"
}